{
  "image_name": "0cf655c2b0d4fd86cf00f9071e15a025.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes two mouse lineage-tracing strategies and how they revise the classical view of blood development. In the upper left, the Sleeping Beauty transposon system uses doxycycline to induce a transposase, which mobilizes a transposon carrying a fluorescent marker into random genomic sites. These insertion sites act as heritable barcodes for tracking descendant cells. In the upper right, the Polylox system uses cell type-specific Cre recombinase to reshuffle a loxP-containing cassette through excision and inversion, generating many distinct recombined DNA barcodes.\n\nThe lower half contrasts a textbook hematopoietic hierarchy with lineage relationships inferred from such barcoding. The classical model shows a tidy branching tree from long-term hematopoietic stem cells through multipotent progenitors to progressively restricted progenitors and finally mature blood cell types. The lineage-tracing view is less orderly: colored circles representing distinct clones are spread unevenly across stem, progenitor, and mature compartments. Some clones contribute strongly to particular outputs, while others span multiple branches, revealing a more heterogeneous and overlapping pattern of differentiation than the simple hierarchical scheme suggests. Overall, the figure emphasizes that genetic barcoding uncovers complex clonal behavior hidden by classical models.",
